WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880
All right. Thank you.

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:04.280
And this is my first time in front of many of

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.875
you so I figured I would give you kind of one slide of introduction.

00:00:06.875 --> 00:00:08.630
I don't want to go too far into what I'm

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:10.485
doing because I know many of you will be here tomorrow.

00:00:10.485 --> 00:00:14.135
And I'm talking, a little bit more broadly about research there.

00:00:14.135 --> 00:00:17.330
But my research is in empirical security and privacy.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.175
A lot of looking at the real world events that are taking place on the internet today.

00:00:21.175 --> 00:00:22.740
And there's kind of been three major themes,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.590
and this one has been building systems to actually

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:27.295
collect data about what's going on on the internet.

00:00:27.295 --> 00:00:29.870
A lot of this has been involved in internet-wide scanning,

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:31.310
understanding how web servers,

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:32.735
mail servers have been deployed.

00:00:32.735 --> 00:00:34.490
But there's also been a lot of working with,

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:37.630
industry groups to understand what they see in their data,

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:40.175
working with groups like Google and Amazon

00:00:40.175 --> 00:00:43.230
and Cloudflare to look at the incoming connections.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.740
So to essentially understand what is going on on the internet.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:46.940
It's massively distributed.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.350
But oftentimes we need this kind of

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.710
global perspective in order to make the right policy decisions,

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.205
in order to understand errors that have been made in deployment,

00:00:55.205 --> 00:00:58.280
and in order to understand how to build kind of the next generation of

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.430
protocols and how they'll fit in with what's already out there.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.790
But actually today, I wanted to actually talk about

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:08.270
an event that kind of I think sets the stage a little bit for today,

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:10.760
and kind of motivation around security.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:16.025
And I actually want to go back to October 21st, 2016.

00:01:16.025 --> 00:01:19.850
And this is a day that many of you may remember, um,

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:23.260
when a lot of these popular websites: Twitter, and Amazon,

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:28.215
and PayPal were unavailable largely on the Eastern Seaboard, but also,

00:01:28.215 --> 00:01:32.920
also on the West Coast kinda all over the U.S. And,

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.420
these sites were unavailable on and off throughout the day.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.130
[LAUGHTER] And this of course led to this kind of rampant speculation that,

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:41.735
the United States was under attack.

00:01:41.735 --> 00:01:43.790
This was another nation state,

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:45.825
trying to take down American infrastructure.

00:01:45.825 --> 00:01:49.075
This was right before the election and people were saying,

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:51.380
"This is a trial run".

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.720
So we've got these kind of crazy,

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.880
uh, different statements from everyone.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.650
And today, I actually wanted to describe what actually

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.650
happened because it's actually kind of interesting.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.900
So what happened that day was that there is a massive denial

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.140
of service attack against a large DNS provider called Dyn,

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.530
Dyn is kind of one of those big plumbing companies

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.950
on the internet that provided DNS for Twitter,

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.580
for Amazon, for these other companies that allowed you to do the look up

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:19.160
from domain to what were these groups' IP addresses.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:22.270
And they came under a massive denial of

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:25.330
service attack that day that essentially overload their network,

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.460
overloaded a lot of their servers.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:31.150
And this was probably the first time we had

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.400
seen an event kind of this- this size or of this scale.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.590
There's actually the third event in a series of four that looks very

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:42.910
different than the previous denial of service attacks we had seen on the internet.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:47.090
The first one actually happened in September of 2016.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:50.680
And it was an attack that hit the European hosting provider OVH.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.320
And then there is another one that hit a blog for

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:57.250
Brian Krebs the security blogger and researcher later in September.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.560
And then in October, this first one hit Dyn,

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.295
and that's when people really started to take notice that there was this large attacks.

00:03:03.295 --> 00:03:07.380
But these kind of four attacks looked very different from what we had seen before.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.800
In these, there were kind of two major differences.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.050
First, was just the sheer magnitude of

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:17.470
the attack this DDoS attack that we saw against Dyn,

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.900
that we saw against Krebs is about twice as large of

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.220
an attack that we had ever seen publicly documented before.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:26.410
So the attack was somewhere between

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:30.340
600 gigabits per second to a terabyte of traffic coming in,

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:35.165
and beforehand we have seen somewhere around 360 gigabits of traffic.

00:03:35.165 --> 00:03:39.820
And the second way, it was different was that we had been very used to seeing

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:43.885
distributed denial of service attacks that were amplified in the past.

00:03:43.885 --> 00:03:47.890
Someone would send essentially spoof the source IP address of a client,

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:49.780
they would send this to a DNS Resolver to

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:53.920
an NTP server to some sort of service that would amplify the request,

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.055
and they would send that amplified request to the target victim.

00:03:57.055 --> 00:04:00.100
And this would mean that even if you only had one gigabit of bandwidth,

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.525
you could still deliver quite a punch with a

00:04:02.525 --> 00:04:06.905
60 or 70 times increase in the amount of data that you were sending.

00:04:06.905 --> 00:04:12.625
And this is what we had been seeing for quite a while in the DDoS space.

00:04:12.625 --> 00:04:18.145
But, what hit these four attacks actually didn't use any amplification.

00:04:18.145 --> 00:04:22.390
There wasn't this 60 to 70 times increase in the traffic.

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:25.010
In fact it was all organic traffic.

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:31.180
It was direct connections from hosts on the internet that were sending GET requests,

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.260
that are sending malformed router packets there.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:39.610
But, but it was 600 gigabytes of true traffic coming from devices [NOISE].

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.370
And what was also different was that the IP addresses we had

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:47.050
seen in this attack are IP addresses we really never seen on the internet before.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:50.260
These were not normal client addresses.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:53.410
In fact they were all IoT devices.

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.620
They were the smallest devices on the internet.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:00.100
These are these are Web cameras and cable modems,

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.470
and these devices that people deploying don't think about,

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:05.620
you don't even think to even really have a CPU.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:10.695
They do they have some small ARM processor or some small Intel processor.

00:05:10.695 --> 00:05:13.690
But the sheer magnitude of the number of these devices.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:15.790
Was able to take down and produce

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:19.420
a much larger attack than we had ever seen on the internet.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:23.870
These devices that were part of

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:28.190
this attack were all infected with a piece of malware called Mirai.

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.535
Which is actually what I want to talk about today.

00:05:30.535 --> 00:05:36.360
And Mirai was kind of the first major piece of malware that's targeted IoT devices.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.490
It's not actually the first,

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:42.685
but the first that was really successful to have this magnitude of attack pulled off.

00:05:42.685 --> 00:05:47.575
The malware itself is pretty simple.

00:05:47.575 --> 00:05:50.320
Bots once are infected go out and they perform

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.665
stateless scanning for other hosts to infect.

00:05:53.665 --> 00:05:56.560
And in the beginning, this was scanning for telnet.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.950
They would search for different any IPv4 address

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:04.210
randomly that had port 23 open and later on they added the second port 23,

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:07.180
23 which we kind of called the IoT telnet port.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:11.215
Just because IoT devices thought they were adding security by,

00:06:11.215 --> 00:06:16.480
by changing the port slightly and they scan for these random hosts,

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:18.520
and then they would attempt to log in with

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:21.725
about five to 10 default usernames and passwords.

00:06:21.725 --> 00:06:27.245
And these were admin like passwords like Admin Admin or root password.

00:06:27.245 --> 00:06:29.560
These were not ingenious combinations.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:32.180
These were if you sat down and wrote five or 10 things on

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:35.575
a paper probably what you would write down.

00:06:35.575 --> 00:06:38.600
So these Bots go off and they scan,

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.150
they find other devices to infect.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:44.660
They report them all back to kind of a central C2 or command

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:48.870
and control server that server then says,

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:54.600
um, essentially it sends out a task to load malware on the machine.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.965
Uh, that mal- that, uh, that loader logs in,

00:06:56.965 --> 00:06:59.750
it deploys a binary that essentially takes over the machine.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:03.620
It kills some of the running processes and it starts up and in.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:10.020
It it converts that IoT device into a bot,

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:13.340
and then that bot kind of takes over and starts to do its own thing,

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:16.790
it starts to scan, it starts to look for other hosts to infect.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:18.860
And it kind of just sits there and waits.

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:22.235
So during this process the bot master is,

00:07:22.235 --> 00:07:23.810
is collecting bots to use it,

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:25.520
it's building its army,

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:30.530
and these bots all sit there until they receive a signal says, "Go off and attack."

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:34.775
And we'll talk about a little later of who they attacked and why.

00:07:34.775 --> 00:07:39.180
But this looks a lot like the malware we had seen on desktop computers.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:44.410
But it was very specifically targeted towards IoT devices.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:53.000
So I mentioned, this wasn't the first piece of malware that went at IoT,

00:07:53.000 --> 00:08:00.455
but it was by far the most successful and it was almost an accident.

00:08:00.455 --> 00:08:02.750
That this, this was successful.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.090
The reason Mirai was so successful,

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:08.460
was that it was so stupidly simple.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:11.550
The authors actually really made pretty much every error

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.625
you can make in a piece of malware.

00:08:14.625 --> 00:08:19.375
Their randomization algorithm actually didn't have full coverage over the IP space.

00:08:19.375 --> 00:08:22.990
They messed up the endianness and IP addresses and blacklisted.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:26.855
What they thought was the U.S. post office actually for

00:08:26.855 --> 00:08:31.150
actually just taking off the random last couple of bits of IP addresses.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:34.430
Their their loading service would drop packets.

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:37.185
Ah, the code didn't really make sense.

00:08:37.185 --> 00:08:40.240
In the beginning, we were, we were fascinated by this because we actually

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.780
thought this was like an obfuscation kind of tactic.

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:47.680
We thought that there was just something we are missing in this.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:51.160
And the answer was no it was just, it was terrible.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:55.870
It looked like code that like a high school student had cobbled together,

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:57.700
and, and tried to run.

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:01.720
Um, but it didn't take more than logging

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:06.190
in was the user name of admin and password to take over these devices.

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:10.210
In fact the reason it was so successful was because it was so simple.

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:12.460
All they needed was a shell or a bash

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:14.905
command and they would run a couple of bash processes.

00:09:14.905 --> 00:09:17.965
But that's what all of these devices have in common.

00:09:17.965 --> 00:09:19.780
All of these different devices from

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:22.660
different manufacturers might have had the username, admin,

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:23.890
and the password admin,

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:28.560
but because they didn't try to go after any vulnerabilities that were specific to devices.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:30.700
They were able just to kind of take

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:34.550
the shotgun approach and they had no idea what they were going to take over.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:37.540
Um, I think it was probably just as much a surprise to them as

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:41.109
it was to us of who they actually were able to infect,

00:09:41.109 --> 00:09:48.320
but it was the simplicity that really led to the success in this, in this ecosystem.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:51.230
So the authors took these existing, uh,

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:54.830
pieces of malware and essentially dumbed them down and added

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:56.930
a little bit more statelessness and they were able to take

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:00.030
over hundreds of thousands of devices with it.

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:08.230
So that's kind of the background of what Mirai was but what actually happened in October.

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:10.430
And I think this was actually a little bit more interesting.

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:14.540
And the first question I think is a very simple one: How large was Mirai?

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:17.390
How many Bots did it actually take over?

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:20.315
And the news sources had numbers all over the place.

00:10:20.315 --> 00:10:23.420
We saw numbers from a half million to a million to millions.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:26.120
Um, and a lot of those are pretty exaggerated,

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:29.830
because a lot of these hosts were in space where there is just a lot of churn.

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:32.855
And so a lot of these, these hosts were being double counted.

00:10:32.855 --> 00:10:37.775
But we were actually able to track pretty specifically the size of the botnet

00:10:37.775 --> 00:10:42.980
because it scanned using this kind of bizarre hacked together approach.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:46.100
That was  actually very finger printable from the outside.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:49.250
They took static values and they put them in all of the fields that should have been

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:52.670
random like your IP ID ind your TCP sequence number.

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:56.810
And you've several of these fields where you'd usually have 32 bits of entropy.

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:59.000
But they just used the same value in each of them.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:02.990
So if you watched the traffic that you saw coming into a large network,

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:08.340
it was pretty obvious what machines were part of Mirai.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:14.400
We actually saw the first scan on August 1st 2016 um,

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:16.520
there is actually isn't an IoT device.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:21.460
It was a server that was rented out of a hosting provider in New Jersey, um,

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:26.140
that was like a a kind of bulletproof hosting provider named DataWagon,

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:28.020
and they scanned for about two hours,

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:31.260
and you can kind of see this the beginning of what's going on,

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.000
then after two hours they stop,

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:35.130
and then we start to see the botnet emerge.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:36.280
So what happened was,

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:39.420
this person essentially ran a scan from a host they controlled,

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:42.290
they found a list of initial IP addresses to go after,

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:44.510
then they went and loaded malware on them,

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:46.055
and then they said okay,

00:11:46.055 --> 00:11:50.810
all of my bots go forth and look for more victims.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:55.645
So, within the first minute of the botnet starting,

00:11:55.645 --> 00:11:58.060
um, there are about 800 devices infected,

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:02.470
this raised to about 11,000 in about 10 minutes,

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:05.600
uh, and then it continues to grow.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:07.960
By the end of the first day about 24 hours,

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:11.655
there's about 65,000 hosts that have been infected,

00:12:11.655 --> 00:12:13.555
and are going out and scanning.

00:12:13.555 --> 00:12:18.300
And again, this is just based on logging in with default usernames and passwords,

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:22.050
I think there's five hard coded in the initial malware.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:25.660
And we see this kind of it says- it remains

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:28.655
small in the beginning and then we see a couple large jumps.

00:12:28.655 --> 00:12:30.170
The first big jump,

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:33.900
you kind of see where the green appears is where they

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:38.105
start to also scan for port 2323 instead of just pure telnet,

00:12:38.105 --> 00:12:40.250
and then you see another large jump,

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:45.030
kind of right at the beginning of December where they start scan for protocol CWMP,

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:49.190
or customer uh, i- it's a protocol

00:12:49.190 --> 00:12:53.290
that lets essentially ISPs control cable modems on their networks.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:56.575
Um so, actually this giant hop like right here is

00:12:56.575 --> 00:13:00.240
like three ISPs or so that just had default usernames and passwords,

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:04.400
had vulnerable cable modems in this space uh,

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:07.335
and then you see that the ISP notices this,

00:13:07.335 --> 00:13:09.945
that all of a sudden their entire ISP doesn't work,

00:13:09.945 --> 00:13:12.035
uh, and it's just scanning,

00:13:12.035 --> 00:13:18.315
and they patch and then you can kind of see this decay as- as folks restart their modems,

00:13:18.315 --> 00:13:22.370
that they come back and take on their normal kind of activity.

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:25.420
So, at the peak here in December,

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:28.615
there is about 600,000 hosts that are infected,

00:13:28.615 --> 00:13:34.605
but the steady state kind of stays somewhere between 200 and 300,000 hosts.

00:13:34.605 --> 00:13:37.065
And then you'll notice kind of towards the end it starts

00:13:37.065 --> 00:13:40.605
to- to decrease and that's not good news actually.

00:13:40.605 --> 00:13:44.060
Um, ah, I'll come back to that a little bit later.

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:49.415
So, Mirai takes over 600,000 of these IoT devices,

00:13:49.415 --> 00:13:53.820
and it's capable of producing about 600 gigabytes of

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:59.920
DDoS traffic taking down these pretty large providers.

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.550
What did Mirai actually take over?

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:05.280
Um, we can actually look at this because most

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.785
of these devices that you could log in over telnet,

00:14:07.785 --> 00:14:12.825
also have web interfaces that are actually very helpfully tell you exactly what they are.

00:14:12.825 --> 00:14:15.050
It might even be the telnet prompt that says,

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:17.250
hey you're logging into uh,

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:20.540
Netgear router, you're logging into this camera.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:21.850
Um, but we can go through,

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:25.245
we can pull out using internet wide scanning what

00:14:25.245 --> 00:14:27.240
all the websites and all of these devices look

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:30.030
like at the time that they were taken over.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:34.310
And from that we can start to understand what these actually were,

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:37.320
and they truly are IoT devices.

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:41.190
We actually see very few devices that were desktop computers or anything like that.

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:44.340
The vast majority were home routers,

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:47.100
they were cameras and DVRs,

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:50.545
they were NASs like network attached storage devices,

00:14:50.545 --> 00:14:53.280
really they were kind of all over the place.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:57.320
It was just happened to be where they saw the passwords that matched up.

00:14:57.320 --> 00:15:00.870
And if you actually look at the passwords that you guess,

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.980
some of them are specific enough that we can track them back to

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:07.790
a specific creator or manufacturers,

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:09.870
in other cases when the passwords one, two,

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:12.610
three, four, five, um,

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:14.990
don't really know who they're targeting, um,

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:17.925
that password is used across a number of manufacturers.

00:15:17.925 --> 00:15:25.790
But you start to see patterns among the types of devices that these are going after.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:35.995
So, I mentioned at the beginning that these are the four kind of large attacks.

00:15:35.995 --> 00:15:38.910
There was OVH, there's Brian Krebs,

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:41.420
there was Dyn, but

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:44.370
those are really kind of the top four because they grabbed our attention.

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:51.450
By deploying honeypots and actually watching the commands that came in to these bots,

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:54.250
we could actually tell who they were going after.

00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:58.630
And it's kind of an eclectic list.

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:01.800
Um, you might be saying to yourself,

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:06.245
what do these Websites really have in common?

00:16:06.245 --> 00:16:11.180
You have quite literally cooking blogs um,

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:16.840
and then you also have Dyn who's a major- major provider on the Internet,

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:19.935
what- what what do these all have in common?

00:16:19.935 --> 00:16:25.150
And the answer is actually pretty much nothing.

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:30.560
And the reason for that is that on September 30th um,

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:32.885
kind of in an underground hacking forum,

00:16:32.885 --> 00:16:36.930
there is this post where the author of Mirai essentially said,

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:40.900
here's the malware, everyone go at it.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:44.925
And this wasn't necessarily uh,

00:16:44.925 --> 00:16:48.265
an act of uh,

00:16:48.265 --> 00:16:50.875
of goodwill on his part,

00:16:50.875 --> 00:16:53.360
it's actually a fairly known kind of tactic of,

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:55.245
if you believe um,

00:16:55.245 --> 00:16:59.590
you're being that- that the police are starting to find who you are,

00:16:59.590 --> 00:17:02.020
one way of getting rid of the evidence is giving it to everyone.

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:06.630
And so then all of a sudden every researcher has it,

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:11.050
every- every nefarious actor has a copy of this malware.

00:17:11.050 --> 00:17:14.680
And when this happened Mirai started to evolve.

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:21.210
So it started as this very dumb piece of malware that scanned five usernames and passwords,

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:25.375
but the moment it went into the forums people started to add features,

00:17:25.375 --> 00:17:27.340
and this is where you see these things,

00:17:27.340 --> 00:17:30.885
like all of a sudden there's this giant spike of the CWMP hosts,

00:17:30.885 --> 00:17:34.115
is because one variant decided they would add one vulnerability,

00:17:34.115 --> 00:17:35.200
they will start to add,

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:42.215
to scan for another type of- of device that they would add different um,

00:17:42.215 --> 00:17:44.400
different protocols, different passwords,

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:46.690
different ranges that they block, uh,

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:48.810
different tactics for how they would

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:51.830
prevent the device from being infected in the future.

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:54.905
And what was really interesting is we actually saw essentially,

00:17:54.905 --> 00:17:57.550
the last 10 years of malware evolution

00:17:57.550 --> 00:18:01.890
occur over the process of or the- the span of a couple of weeks.

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:06.820
So, we've had this long history of desktop malware getting smarter and smarter,

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:09.235
starting to use domain generation algorithms,

00:18:09.235 --> 00:18:13.470
starting to remove other viruses that are on that machine so that you have full control,

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:17.380
and we saw the exact same thing happen when this was released.

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:21.560
So, we went from this really dumb piece of malware within a couple of weeks to

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:25.815
essentially a pretty sophisticated looking piece of malware.

00:18:25.815 --> 00:18:30.850
And that's actually starts to lead into why we saw this kind of downward slope.

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:35.520
But as this happened the other thing that happened was that you also had now

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:40.785
had different factions that were controlling different parts of the botnet,

00:18:40.785 --> 00:18:44.210
and they actually start to fight each other.

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:49.265
Um, so, when you actually look at who was being attacked what you'd notice would be that,

00:18:49.265 --> 00:18:52.020
in one of the clusters they would be

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:55.545
attacking the command and control servers of another cluster,

00:18:55.545 --> 00:18:57.450
cause the idea was if that-

00:18:57.450 --> 00:19:01.110
that command control server went down you could take over its bots,

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:05.565
that they'd eventually reboot and try to find a new master and you would take them over.

00:19:05.565 --> 00:19:08.495
And so, there is this large evolution,

00:19:08.495 --> 00:19:12.100
but in the end there ended up being a couple of very large clusters.

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:17.170
And what I have up here on the screen essentially is a clustering

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:22.815
of the command and control infrastructure associated with different clusters.

00:19:22.815 --> 00:19:25.500
So, we go in and we look at all the DNS names,

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:26.600
and the IP addresses,

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:31.490
and the ownership of these different resources at ISPs at these DNS providers,

00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:34.500
and we are able to link these together by saying,

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:37.320
these three names points to this IP address,

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:39.865
one of them migrated over to another IP address,

00:19:39.865 --> 00:19:42.830
we can start to piece together the story.

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:46.020
But this also starts to tell a story of,

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:51.390
why did we see such a weird range of Websites being attacked?

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:55.955
So, the first cluster that you see this green one is actually the original one,

00:19:55.955 --> 00:19:58.370
is the one that went after Brian Krebs,

00:19:58.370 --> 00:20:00.300
it's the one that went over OVH.

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:02.795
You see cluster two which is,

00:20:02.795 --> 00:20:06.905
looks like the largest which actually is what we call booter service,

00:20:06.905 --> 00:20:11.230
essentially the- the owners of that put up a website and they would say,

00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:14.745
if you give me a bitcoin I will DoS your enemy.

00:20:14.745 --> 00:20:16.890
And there's actually this full marketplace,

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:19.685
you can go and you can actually bid and say,

00:20:19.685 --> 00:20:20.780
this is how much bitcoin,

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:24.430
or how much other money I want to spend on this attack, and then you'll say,

00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:31.725
I want to purchase one hour of DDoS against my- my competitor's cooking blog.

00:20:31.725 --> 00:20:38.790
And there's been a large ecosystem of this in kind of the- the- the amplification space,

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:48.570
but we again saw the same thing emerge here in the- in the IoT space.

00:20:48.570 --> 00:20:51.045
There still isn't necessarily an obvious question,

00:20:51.045 --> 00:20:58.280
like answer of why attack both Brian Krebs and Dyn and OVH?

00:20:58.280 --> 00:21:02.130
Like why- why go after these large,

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:04.560
these large hosting providers?

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:08.010
And- and why if you can't put together code,

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:13.450
do you think I'm going to go after one of the largest DNS providers in the country?

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:18.480
And the acture- answer is Minecraft actually.

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:26.105
Um, one of the reasons that these denial service kind of groups come up is that,

00:21:26.105 --> 00:21:28.785
there's a lot of money to be made running a Minecraft server,

00:21:28.785 --> 00:21:32.930
and it's very easy to get folks to move over to another Minecraft server.

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:34.770
It's not like you have to move all your infrastructure from

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:36.820
EC2 to Google Cloud or something like that.

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:39.270
Gamers say this server is down as long as all of us

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:42.665
move to the same other provider, let's move.

00:21:42.665 --> 00:21:47.460
And so, this is being used- using for blackmail,

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:53.940
they were actually extorting the owners of very large Minecraft servers on OVH.

00:21:54.580 --> 00:22:00.010
Um, for Dyn, it was actually going after PlayStation servers.

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:04.455
Dyn hosts the authoritative DNS records for PlayStation.

00:22:04.455 --> 00:22:06.580
And so while what we see from the outside looks like

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:09.070
an attack that's going after Dyn as a whole,

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:14.625
the fact that Dyn went down was actually just an unfortunate circumstance.

00:22:14.625 --> 00:22:21.695
These attackers are really just going after uh, these PlayStation servers.

00:22:21.695 --> 00:22:24.750
Um, and if- if you DoS correctly you can- you can

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:27.360
you have an advantage in this game um,

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:30.060
because your other, the other players don't know if this is coming,

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:32.565
they can't time their moves.

00:22:32.565 --> 00:22:35.220
And it had nothing to do with an attack

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:38.400
beyond just going after a couple of these servers.

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:41.120
[NOISE]

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:43.610
For Brian Krebs, um,

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:45.605
they were actually just pissed at him.

00:22:45.605 --> 00:22:50.725
Um, they were really upset that he had written articles that kind of, uh, had, er,

00:22:50.725 --> 00:22:53.100
uncovered a similar booter operation,

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:58.140
and it was run by their friends and they decided to go after him for this.

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:03.355
Uh, and that retribution was the largest attack we had seen on the Internet.

00:23:03.355 --> 00:23:06.985
But again it was, they were annoyed their friend had been,

00:23:06.985 --> 00:23:10.805
had been arrested because of an article that Brian had written.

00:23:10.805 --> 00:23:13.440
Like that's where we are.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:17.580
We took down the largest like DNS provider,

00:23:17.580 --> 00:23:22.820
because a couple of folks were really upset about a blog post.

00:23:23.200 --> 00:23:27.600
Tha- that's where we are in IoT space right now.

00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:31.350
So, who are these people?

00:23:31.350 --> 00:23:35.090
Um, as I mentioned [LAUGHTER]

00:23:35.090 --> 00:23:39.005
the authors of thi- this did some really stupid stupid things.

00:23:39.005 --> 00:23:40.910
And we actually know who did it.

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:45.945
Um, they were actually arrested in the end of 2017.

00:23:45.945 --> 00:23:50.120
Um, these were a couple of college students.

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:54.870
They were undergrads.

00:23:54.870 --> 00:24:01.660
Um, they had started a business operation where they would blackmail the owners of,

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:03.385
of these Minecraft servers.

00:24:03.385 --> 00:24:04.880
That was their business.

00:24:04.880 --> 00:24:07.375
They were with- they would send so many emails saying,

00:24:07.375 --> 00:24:11.310
"If you don't move over to our superior DDoS protection service,

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:13.195
your website's gonna go offline."

00:24:13.195 --> 00:24:18.125
Really, they just wouldn't DoS them if, if they paid up.

00:24:18.125 --> 00:24:21.815
And this just spiraled, and others, uh,

00:24:21.815 --> 00:24:24.430
took control, once they had released the malware,

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:26.900
and started to build on top of it.

00:24:30.570 --> 00:24:35.385
Mirai has a somewhat ironic name,

00:24:35.385 --> 00:24:36.450
or maybe it's depressing,

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:39.660
I don't know of the future.

00:24:39.890 --> 00:24:43.980
Uh, and I, I don't know if this was,

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:47.900
necessarily know what the authors meant when they named this, this.

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:51.250
Um, but it does kind of raise this interesting question

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:54.310
of this likely is kind of the future,

00:24:54.310 --> 00:24:56.890
unless we start to clean up this act.

00:24:56.890 --> 00:24:59.910
Um, and it's not just policy.

00:24:59.910 --> 00:25:02.655
Most of these devices actually were not in the United States.

00:25:02.655 --> 00:25:05.810
Most of them were actually in, uh,

00:25:05.810 --> 00:25:08.170
checking, I don't have the slide,

00:25:08.170 --> 00:25:10.510
but I can pull it up.

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:15.080
Um, uh, there's this bias here,

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:16.370
actually you can't see this, can you?

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:17.630
[NOISE] Let's see.

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:22.660
Um, [NOISE] if I can show this to you.

00:25:22.660 --> 00:25:26.140
Most of these devices were not in the United States.

00:25:26.140 --> 00:25:29.070
Um, these were in regions that we

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:31.980
didn't really consider having significant amounts of bandwidth.

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:33.110
They were in South America,

00:25:33.110 --> 00:25:34.420
they were in Southeast Asia.

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:41.465
Um, and the reason that this malware looked to kind of tail off at the end,

00:25:41.465 --> 00:25:44.145
isn't that these devices have been patched.

00:25:44.145 --> 00:25:47.010
It's actually that the malware has gotten more sophisticated.

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:49.270
That new botnets have taken over,

00:25:49.270 --> 00:25:53.205
and they are using much harder to detect me-,

00:25:53.205 --> 00:25:56.935
uh, methods of, of taking over the other hosts.

00:25:56.935 --> 00:25:59.030
They're starting to use, uh,

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:01.910
attacks that aren't just usernames, and passwords guessing.

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:05.945
And we haven't really seen attacks from these new mal- from these new pieces of malware,

00:26:05.945 --> 00:26:08.770
from these new botnets that are taken over from Mirai,

00:26:08.770 --> 00:26:12.855
but we do know that they are slowly ramping up in size,

00:26:12.855 --> 00:26:15.965
and they are capable of,

00:26:15.965 --> 00:26:18.800
of starting these attacks, that I mean,

00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:22.310
as part of this Akamai said to Brian Krebs, "You go- you have to move off.

00:26:22.310 --> 00:26:24.290
This is affecting our production network.

00:26:24.290 --> 00:26:28.240
Um, that there's only a couple of providers in the world that can actually

00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:33.580
withstand the attacks that are now possible from these IoT devices,

00:26:33.580 --> 00:26:37.015
from this, this kind of, uh, lack of,

00:26:37.015 --> 00:26:39.135
of attention into the security,

00:26:39.135 --> 00:26:43.650
and this kind of rush to the bottom to get these devices out and deployed."

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:50.555
The work, uh, presented here was part of a very large team across industry,

00:26:50.555 --> 00:26:52.830
across, uh, uh, law enforcement,

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:55.115
and as well as academic users, uh,

00:26:55.115 --> 00:26:58.760
to look at the traffic that was actually seen at Dyn,

00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:00.610
seen at Akamai, seen at Google,

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:03.700
to look at the DNS infrastructure behind these attacks.

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:05.580
Um, but there is a paper out there,

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:07.565
if this is of interest to other folks to kind of

00:27:07.565 --> 00:27:09.870
understand more in depth what was going on.

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:13.130
And with that I'll take any questions.

